WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.880
Imagine you're at a party, there
are a couple of dozen guests,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.840
what are the chances that two of them
will have the same birthday?

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.960
The answer is, better than even.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.000
If the party had 60 guests,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.400
then chances would
be greater than 99%.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.680
It's an example of how we often
are too impressed by coincidences,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.520
because we forget about
how many ways there are

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.400
for coincidences to occur.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.360
The tendency to see
meaningful connections

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.560
between completely unrelated things

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.480
is what psychologists call apophenia.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.640
It means we often see patterns
in random information,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.480
and this can be deeply problematic.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.520
When we hallucinate things
that aren't there,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.520
we can make foolish decisions.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.360
We can imagine that
there are conspiracies

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.040
because several bad things
happen in a row.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.840
We can believe in malevolent deities

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.960
because we underestimate how easy
it is for misfortunes to cluster.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.680
We can be victims
of the 'gambler's fallacy',

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.360
and think, for example, that if the
roulette wheel has landed on red

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.960
six times in a row,
it's due for a black,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.480
even though, of course, the roulette
wheel doesn't have a memory

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.160
and a desire to play fair.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.080
The 'gambler's fallacy', also known
as the 'Monte Carlo fallacy',

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.080
is the incorrect belief that
a past event will influence

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.160
the outcome of a future event.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.360
So why do so many of us fall for it?

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.440
The whole point of having a brain

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.800
is to figure out what's
going on in the world.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.680
That's a useful thing
in a natural environment,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:41.880
because we don't have a direct wire
from our brain to reality,

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.680
we're always interpreting patterns.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.760
We see shapes hidden,
partly obscured by leaves,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.200
we see fish under the surface
of the water,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.560
we always go beyond
the information given.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.080
But it does mean that
we can overshoot

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.840
and interpret things
that aren't actually there.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.040
Not just in the realm
of visual shapes,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.920
but in the realm of events.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.520
This failure to appreciate randomness
can lead to all sorts of problems.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.120
It can lead some people
to dismiss climate change

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.080
after a record cold day,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.280
when in fact, if they were
able to step back

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.800
and look at the overall trend,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.400
it would be clear that it was
a completely normal,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.360
if random, fluctuation.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.400
And that can lead us
to attribute meaning

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.560
to totally unrelated life events.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.560
When events happen randomly in time,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.040
they often fall into clusters,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.000
because there is no process
that's trying to space them apart.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.760
That's something that is very hard
for us to appreciate.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.800
Now when it comes
to events in our lives

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.360
that might be distributed
randomly in time,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.320
from the perspective of our minds
seeking patterns,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.120
they seem to come into clusters,
and so we may believe

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.280
that bad things happen in threes,
that God is testing our faith,

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.760
that we're born under a bad sign.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.680
Another common illusion
is prior probability,

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.480
sometimes called the
'Texas sharpshooter fallacy',

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.480
after the marksman who fires a bullet
into the side of a barn

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.240
and then draws a bullseye
around the hole afterwards.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.400
There's the story about
the stock market adviser

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.920
who sends out several
thousand newsletters,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.880
half of which predicted
that the market will go up,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.600
and half that predicted
it would go down,

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.280
he then discards the names
on the mailing list

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.760
who happen to get the
incorrect prediction.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.880
Well, you can see
where this is going.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:31.240
After a year, there'll be a certain
remainder who will think this guy

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.240
is a genius.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.440
It's a fallacy that
we are all prone to,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.320
it's why we are often impressed
by psychics and soothsayers

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.560
whose predictions
are amazingly accurate,

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.280
after they've occurred, we tend
to forget all the false predictions.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.800
So if we know that we are prone
to falling for these delusions,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.080
to seeing patterns that aren't there,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.560
what can we do about it?

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.160
An awareness that we are
all vulnerable to fallacies

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.000
and illusions, biases, doesn't mean
we should just fatalistically

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.720
throw up our hands and say
humans are irrational

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.920
and therefore we need some kind
of benevolent despots

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.400
to make decisions for us,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.400
that democracy was a big mistake.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.440
Probably none of us is that wise

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.560
that we can notice
our own fallacious thinking,

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.840
we're often very better at noticing
the other guy's fallacious thinking.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.480
And we can harness that ability

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.280
in communities that have free speech,
open debate, an adversarial process,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.400
checks and balances,
editing, fact checking,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.560
so that one person's
mistaken first impression,

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.960
their illusory snap judgement, 

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.840
can be spotted by someone else,
and the community as a whole

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.160
implements the most
reasoned decision.

